  "ipBasicsToc8": "8) Mini exercises (quickly verify a mask / a network)",
  "ipBasicsToc9": "9) Quick checklist",

  "ipBasicsSection1Paragraph": "An IP address (IPv4) is a logical identifier on a network. It tells the network: “send this packet to this device”.\n\nWhen you connect a console, an Art-Net/sACN node, a PC, a managed switch… everything that talks on the network must have a coherent IP so devices can find each other.",
  "ipBasicsSection1CalloutTitle": "Mental picture",
  "ipBasicsSection1Bullet1": "MAC = hardware identifier (network card).",
  "ipBasicsSection1Bullet2": "IP = “postal address” on a network (changeable).",
  "ipBasicsSection1Bullet3": "Mask = “district / ZIP code” (defines the subnet).",
  "ipBasicsSection1Bullet4": "Gateway = “way out of the district” (router).",
  "ipBasicsSection1Subtitle": "IPv4 in 10 seconds",
  "ipBasicsSection1SubBullet1": "Format: A.B.C.D (4 numbers from 0 to 255).",
  "ipBasicsSection1SubBullet2": "Ex: 192.168.0.50",
  "ipBasicsSection1SubBullet3": "It is not magic: these are 32 bits (4 octets).",
  "ipBasicsSection1SubBullet4": "What matters for you: IP + mask must be coherent between devices.",

  "ipBasicsSection2Paragraph": "Some IPv4 address ranges are reserved for private networks (LAN). They are not routed on the Internet. That is why we use them in show environments: you can build your local network without “using up” public IP addresses.",
  "ipBasicsSection2CalloutTitle": "The 3 main private ranges (LAN)",
  "ipBasicsSection2Bullet1": "10.0.0.0 → 10.255.255.255 (often written “10.x.x.x”)",
  "ipBasicsSection2Bullet2": "172.16.0.0 → 172.31.255.255 (often “172.16–31.x.x”)",
  "ipBasicsSection2Bullet3": "192.168.0.0 → 192.168.255.255 (often “192.168.x.x”)",
  "ipBasicsSection2Subtitle1": "Why do we often see 192.168.x.x?",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet1": "Consumer routers/boxes often use 192.168.0.x or 192.168.1.x.",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet2": "It has become a de facto standard for small LANs.",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet3": "But in show setups, 10.x.x.x is practical if you want many addresses or several VLANs.",
  "ipBasicsSection2Subtitle2": "And the famous 2.x.x.x (Art-Net)?",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet4": "A lot of Art-Net hardware ships with a 2.x.x.x IP by default (closed network).",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet5": "This is convenient in plug&play mode (without DHCP), but surprising if your PC is on 192.168.x.x.",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet6": "Important: “2.x” itself is not magic for broadcast/unicast.",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet7": "What changes the broadcast scope is the mask (often /8 = 255.0.0.0 in legacy mode).",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet8": "Example: network 2.0.0.0/8 → broadcast = 2.255.255.255 (very wide).",
  "ipBasicsSection2SubBullet9": "Simple fix: put PC/console in the same range + same mask, or reconfigure the node to a 192/10 plan.",

  "ipBasicsSection3Paragraph": "The mask (subnet mask) tells which addresses are “in the same network”. Without a mask, an IP address does not mean much.\n\nField rule: if two devices are in the same subnet, they talk directly. Otherwise, they need a gateway (router).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Callout1Title": "Most common masks",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet1": "255.0.0.0 ( /8 )   → typical “large” 10.x.x.x network",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet2": "255.255.0.0 ( /16 ) → “large” 10.10.x.x or 192.168.x.x network",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet3": "255.255.255.0 ( /24 ) → classic 192.168.0.x network (254 devices)",
  "ipBasicsSection3Callout2Title": "Mask → broadcast address (very useful to understand)",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet4": "Broadcast depends on the subnet, therefore on the mask (not on “2 vs 192”).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet5": "192.168.10.0/24 → broadcast = 192.168.10.255 (small network).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet6": "2.0.0.0/8 (255.0.0.0) → broadcast = 2.255.255.255 (huge network).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet7": "The “bigger” the network, the noisier a broadcast can be (if you use it often).",
  "ipBasicsSection3Subtitle1": "Concrete example (most useful): /24",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet1": "IP: 192.168.0.50",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet2": "Mask: 255.255.255.0 ( /24 )",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet3": "Network: 192.168.0.0",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet4": "Devices “in the same network”: 192.168.0.1 → 192.168.0.254",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet5": "If you move to 192.168.1.50 → it is NO LONGER the same network.",
  "ipBasicsSection3Subtitle2": "Why create subnets?",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet6": "Organization: separate “lighting”, “video”, “intercom”, “internet”…",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet7": "Performance: limit noise (broadcast/multicast) to one group.",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet8": "Security: prevent a guest PC from seeing/disturbing the show network.",
  "ipBasicsSection3SubBullet9": "Diagnostics: easier to know “where” a device is.",
  "ipBasicsSection3Callout3Title": "Classic trap",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet8": "Same IP “beginning” ≠ same network if the mask is not identical.",
  "ipBasicsSection3Bullet9": "Ex: 10.0.0.5/8 and 10.0.0.200/24 → not the same logical scope.",

  "ipBasicsSection4Subtitle1": "DHCP (automatic assignment)",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet1": "A DHCP server “gives” IP + mask + gateway + DNS.",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet2": "Convenient: plug in and it works.",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet3": "Show risk: if DHCP fails / changes / conflicts → strange symptoms.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Subtitle2": "Static (fixed IP)",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet4": "You define IP + mask (and optionally gateway/DNS).",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet5": "Very useful for: console, nodes, managed switch, show Wi-Fi AP.",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet6": "Risk: duplicate IP if the plan is unclear (conflict).",
  "ipBasicsSection4Callout1Title": "Gateway",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet1": "This is the router address: it is used to go “outside the local network”.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet2": "If you build an isolated show network (without Internet) → gateway is often unnecessary.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet3": "But if you need to reach another VLAN / the Internet → it is essential.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Callout2Title": "DNS",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet4": "DNS = “directory”: translates a name (ex: example.com) into an IP.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet5": "In show environments (Art-Net/sACN), it is often useless: you work with direct IPs.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Bullet6": "If you use services (updates, licenses, time server, streaming) → DNS becomes useful again.",
  "ipBasicsSection4Subtitle3": "Simple rule (beginner-friendly)",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet7": "Small closed show: static everywhere, no gateway, no DNS.",
  "ipBasicsSection4SubBullet8": "Show with Internet / several networks: controlled DHCP + reservations, or static plan + router.",

  "ipBasicsSection5Paragraph": "When A wants to talk to B, it checks the mask:\n• If B is in the same subnet → A sends directly on the local network.\n• If B is outside the subnet → A sends to the gateway (router), which handles the rest.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Callout1Title": "Example (easy)",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet1": "A = 192.168.0.10 /24",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet2": "B = 192.168.0.50 /24 → same network → direct OK.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet3": "B = 192.168.1.50 /24 → different network → router/gateway needed.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Subtitle": "Why won’t it ping?",
  "ipBasicsSection5SubBullet1": "Different mask → A thinks B is “elsewhere” (or vice versa).",
  "ipBasicsSection5SubBullet2": "No gateway → impossible to leave the subnet.",
  "ipBasicsSection5SubBullet3": "PC firewall → ping blocked (very common on Windows).",
  "ipBasicsSection5SubBullet4": "Cable / switch / VLAN: physically not in the same segment.",
  "ipBasicsSection5Callout2Title": "Broadcast vs unicast (useful bonus)",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet4": "Unicast: to one specific IP (clean).",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet5": "Broadcast: “to everyone” on the subnet (noisy).",
  "ipBasicsSection5Bullet6": "In show environments: broadcast can work on a small setup, but quickly becomes noisy on larger networks.",

  "ipBasicsSection6Subtitle1": "Plan A — ultra simple (one /24 network)",
  "ipBasicsSection6Code1": "Network: 192.168.10.0/24   (mask 255.255.255.0)\n\nConsole: 192.168.10.10\nPC (control): 192.168.10.20\nNode 1: 192.168.10.101\nNode 2: 192.168.10.102\nManaged switch: 192.168.10.2\nGateway: (empty) or 192.168.10.1 if router present",
  "ipBasicsSection6Callout1Title": "Why it works well",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet1": "Everyone is in the same subnet → no surprises.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet2": "Easy to explain, easy to troubleshoot.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet3": "Ideal for beginners / small to medium show.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Subtitle2": "Plan B — “10.x” when you want to grow",
  "ipBasicsSection6Code2": "Network: 10.10.0.0/16   (mask 255.255.0.0)\n\nConsole: 10.10.0.10\nPC: 10.10.0.20\nNodes: 10.10.1.10 → 10.10.1.200\nSwitch: 10.10.0.2",
  "ipBasicsSection6Callout2Title": "When to use it",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet4": "When you have many devices (or several areas).",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet5": "When you plan VLANs later (lighting/video/IT).",
  "ipBasicsSection6Subtitle3": "Plan C — Art-Net “2.x.x.x” legacy compatibility",
  "ipBasicsSection6Code3": "Network: 2.0.0.0/8   (mask 255.0.0.0)\n\nPC/console: 2.0.0.10\nNode 1: 2.0.0.100\nNode 2: 2.0.0.101\n\n⚠️ Isolated network, no Internet, no routing.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Callout3Title": "Warning",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet6": "This plan is convenient for old/default hardware.",
  "ipBasicsSection6Bullet7": "But avoid mixing it with a “normal” LAN (192.168.x.x) without a router.",

  "ipBasicsSection7Callout1Title": "Symptoms → likely causes",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet1": "“I see the node but it won’t respond” → different mask / VLAN / firewall.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet2": "“A device works then disappears” → unstable DHCP / IP conflict.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet3": "“Nothing works except on the switch” → IP not in the same network / wrong cable / wrong VLAN port.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet4": "“Everything is slow / packet loss” → broadcast/multicast too wide / low-end switch / network loop.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet5": "“It works wired but not on Wi-Fi” → jitter / roaming / saturated band / power saving.",
  "ipBasicsSection7Subtitle": "Pro method in 5 minutes",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet1": "1) Check console/PC IP + mask and node IP + mask.",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet2": "2) Verify: same subnet? (ex: 192.168.10.x /24)",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet3": "3) Ping (if possible) + test access to the node web interface.",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet4": "4) If DHCP: list who has which IP (DHCP table) and check for duplicates.",
  "ipBasicsSection7SubBullet5": "5) If sACN multicast: check IGMP snooping/querier (otherwise flood).",
  "ipBasicsSection7Callout2Title": "Very common trap",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet6": "Legacy mask on a PC (255.255.0.0 instead of 255.255.255.0).",
  "ipBasicsSection7Bullet7": "Result: you think you are “in the same network”, but not in the same way as the other device.",

  "ipBasicsSection8Subtitle1": "Exercise 1: same network or not? (/24)",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet1": "A = 192.168.10.50 /24",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet2": "B = 192.168.10.200 /24 → ✅ same network",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet3": "B = 192.168.11.200 /24 → ❌ not the same network",
  "ipBasicsSection8Subtitle2": "Exercise 2: /16 (255.255.0.0)",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet4": "A = 10.10.5.10 /16",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet5": "B = 10.10.200.20 /16 → ✅ same network (10.10.x.x)",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet6": "B = 10.11.200.20 /16 → ❌ not the same network",
  "ipBasicsSection8CalloutTitle": "Visual trick",
  "ipBasicsSection8Bullet1": "/24 → same first 3 numbers (A.B.C) = same network.",
  "ipBasicsSection8Bullet2": "/16 → same first 2 numbers (A.B) = same network.",
  "ipBasicsSection8Bullet3": "/8 → same first number (A) = same network.",
  "ipBasicsSection8Subtitle3": "Exercise 3: avoid “weird” IPs",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet7": "0.x.x.x : reserved (not a normal host IP).",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet8": "127.x.x.x : loopback (your PC itself).",
  "ipBasicsSection8SubBullet9": "169.254.x.x : auto-IP when DHCP is absent (APIPA) → sign of a DHCP problem.",

  "ipBasicsSection9CalloutTitle": "Before overcomplicating it",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet1": "Same subnet = coherent IP + mask.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet2": "No duplicate IP (conflict).",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet3": "DHCP: OK if controlled; otherwise clear static plan.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet4": "PC firewall: can block ping / discovery.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet5": "Switch/VLAN: correct port, correct VLAN, no loop.",
  "ipBasicsSection9Bullet6": "sACN multicast: IGMP snooping/querier recommended.",

  "ipBasicsChecklistCopy": "Network — Quick checklist (IP)\n☐ All devices are in the same subnet (coherent IP + mask)\n☐ No duplicate IP (conflict)\n☐ DHCP: either everyone on DHCP, or a clear static plan (avoid fuzzy mixes)\n☐ For Art-Net/sACN: dedicated network or VLAN if possible\n☐ Managed switch if multicast/IGMP (sACN)\n☐ Wi-Fi: avoid for critical show data (jitter), prefer wired\n☐ Simple test: ping + verify mask/gateway",
  "ipBasicsFooterNote": "Goal: understand enough to cable / configure / troubleshoot quickly in show environments.\nNetwork details can go deeper, but these basics cover 90% of field failures.",
  "aboutElectricitePageTitle": "Electrical — field reference",

  "aboutNetworkPageTitle": "Lighting network — VLAN, IGMP, Wi-Fi",
  "aboutNetworkCopyTooltip": "Copy notes",
  "aboutNetworkChecklistCopyTooltip": "Copy",
  "aboutNetworkChecklistTitle": "Before opening Wireshark 😄",
  "aboutNetworkFooterNote": "Indicative field info. Depending on the network hardware, IGMP/VLAN may be essential.\nGoal: stay simple and robust.",

  "aboutNetworkCopy1": "Lighting network: aim for simple, dedicated, and stable.",
  "aboutNetworkCopy2": "VLAN = separate traffic (lighting vs everything else).",
  "aboutNetworkCopy3": "IGMP = essential for large-scale sACN multicast.",
  "aboutNetworkCopy4": "Wi-Fi = OK for troubleshooting, rarely OK for critical production.",

  "aboutNetworkChecklistCopy": "Lighting network — Checklist\n☐ Dedicated network or lighting VLAN\n☐ Clear IP plan (DHCP or static) + correct mask\n☐ Proper switch; loops avoided\n☐ sACN multicast: IGMP snooping ON\n☐ IGMP querier present in the VLAN (if the infrastructure requires it)\n☐ Unicast for troubleshooting (especially Art-Net)\n☐ Wi-Fi: avoid for critical production (jitter/loss)\n☐ Tests: stable ping + progressive load (universes)",

