  "aboutSacnSection1Pill2": "Multicast",
  "aboutSacnSection1Pill3": "Priority",
  "aboutSacnSection1Paragraph": "sACN (E1.31) is a widely used standard for transporting DMX universes over IP. It is often preferred when you want a clean network architecture: properly managed multicast, standardized priorities, and a more “pro network” behavior.",
  "aboutSacnSection1Title": "When it shines",
  "aboutSacnSection1Bullet1": "Many universes on a well-managed network.",
  "aboutSacnSection1Bullet2": "Installations / large networks with multiple sources (priorities).",
  "aboutSacnSection1Bullet3": "When you want to avoid “broadcast everywhere”.",

  "aboutSacnSection2Pill1": "Universe 1..63999",
  "aboutSacnSection2Pill2": "512 slots/universe",
  "aboutSacnSection2Paragraph": "An sACN universe corresponds to a DMX universe: up to 512 slots. Standard sACN numbering is 1..63999. In practice, what matters is console ↔ node ↔ DMX port mapping.",
  "aboutSacnSection2Title": "Common mistake",
  "aboutSacnSection2Bullet1": "You change the universe number on the console side, but not on the node side (or vice versa).",
  "aboutSacnSection2Bullet2": "You patch “Universe 0” while the hardware expects base 1.",
  "aboutSacnSection2Bullet3": "You mix numbering conventions between tools.",

  "aboutSacnSection3Paragraph": "sACN often uses multicast: each universe “lives” on a multicast group, and nodes subscribe to the universes they need.",
  "aboutSacnSection3Title": "IGMP snooping (why it matters)",
  "aboutSacnSection3Bullet1": "Without IGMP: the switch floods multicast everywhere → possible overload.",
  "aboutSacnSection3Bullet2": "With IGMP: the switch sends only to subscribed ports → stable network.",
  "aboutSacnSection3Bullet3": "At large scale: IGMP is often THE difference between “it works” and “it’s hell”.",
  "aboutSacnSection3Subtitle": "Diagram: multicast & IGMP",
  "aboutSacnSection3Subtitle2": "Unicast (option)",
  "aboutSacnSection3SubBullet1": "Possible depending on consoles/nodes.",
  "aboutSacnSection3SubBullet2": "Useful for troubleshooting or if multicast networking is not under control.",
  "aboutSacnSection3SubBullet3": "Less elegant but sometimes simpler.",

  "aboutSacnSection4Pill1": "Source A prio 100",
  "aboutSacnSection4Pill2": "Source B prio 90",
  "aboutSacnSection4Paragraph": "sACN supports priority: if multiple sources send the same universe, the receiver keeps the highest-priority source. Very useful with a main console + backup console + media server.",
  "aboutSacnSection4Title": "Pitfalls",
  "aboutSacnSection4Bullet1": "Two unwanted sources on the same universe → “they fight”.",
  "aboutSacnSection4Bullet2": "Priority set incorrectly → backup takes over.",
  "aboutSacnSection4Bullet3": "Debug: mute one source and check who wins.",

  "aboutSacnSection5Pill1": "IGMP",
  "aboutSacnSection5Pill2": "Switch",
  "aboutSacnSection5Pill3": "No Wi-Fi",
  "aboutSacnSection5Pill4": "Node CPU",
  "aboutSacnSection5Title": "What breaks first",
  "aboutSacnSection5Bullet1": "Multicast without IGMP → flooding.",
  "aboutSacnSection5Bullet2": "Unsuitable switch (buffers/CPU) → loss.",
  "aboutSacnSection5Bullet3": "Wi-Fi → jitter/loss.",
  "aboutSacnSection5Bullet4": "Limited nodes (ports, firmware).",
  "aboutSacnSection5Subtitle": "Simple rules",
  "aboutSacnSection5SubBullet1": "Managed switch + IGMP snooping if many universes.",
  "aboutSacnSection5SubBullet2": "Dedicated lighting VLAN if possible.",
  "aboutSacnSection5SubBullet3": "Use unicast for troubleshooting isolation.",

  "aboutSacnSection6Paragraph": "As with Art-Net, RDM “over IP” often depends on a proxy mechanism in the nodes. The sACN standard does not automatically mean your node handles RDM correctly.",
  "aboutSacnSection6Title": "Check in the node documentation",
  "aboutSacnSection6Bullet1": "RDM proxy / RDM over IP support.",
  "aboutSacnSection6Bullet2": "Limitations (discovery only, partial commands).",
  "aboutSacnSection6Bullet3": "Compatibility with splitters/opto on the DMX side.",
