  "aboutArtnetPageTitle": "Art-Net — DMX universes over IP (simple & complete)",
  "aboutArtnetTocTitle": "Table of contents",
  "aboutArtnetTocCopyTooltip": "Copy notes",
  "aboutArtnetCopyLine1": "Art-Net = DMX over Ethernet via UDP.",
  "aboutArtnetCopyLine2": "Scale: many universes via distributed nodes (DMX-out).",
  "aboutArtnetCopyLine3": "Transport: broadcast (simple but floods) or unicast (cleaner).",
  "aboutArtnetCopyLine4": "Real limits: Wi-Fi, basic switch, node firmware, universe/mapping settings.",
  "aboutArtnetCopyLine5": "RDM: possible via “RDM proxy” depending on nodes (support varies).",
  "aboutArtnetSection1Title": "1) Art-Net — what it is and why",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Title": "2) Addressing & universes — clean mapping",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Title": "3) Limits & performance — what breaks first",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Title": "4) Nodes, splitters & RDM",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Title": "5) Troubleshooting — symptoms → causes",
  "aboutArtnetSection6Title": "6) Diagrams (network / unicast vs broadcast)",
  "aboutArtnetSection6bTitle": "6b) Images (assets) — RJ45/switch/cables",
  "aboutArtnetSection7Title": "7) Quick checklist",
  "aboutArtnetConversionTablesTitle": "Conversion / tables (Universe 1..256)",
  "aboutArtnetPillUdp": "UDP",
  "aboutArtnetPillUniversIp": "DMX universes over IP",
  "aboutArtnetPillNodesDmxOut": "DMX-out nodes",
  "aboutArtnetSection1Paragraph": "Art-Net carries DMX universes over an Ethernet (IP) network. Instead of running one DMX line per universe, you send packets over the network and a node converts them to DMX near the fixtures.",
  "aboutArtnetSection1CalloutTitle": "Why it is useful",
  "aboutArtnetSection1Bullet1": "Scalable: many universes on a single network cable.",
  "aboutArtnetSection1Bullet2": "Distribution: nodes close to fixtures/dimmers → fewer long DMX runs.",
  "aboutArtnetSection1Bullet3": "Flexible patching: changing a universe or moving a node is quick.",
  "aboutArtnetSection1Subtitle": "Two things to remember",
  "aboutArtnetSection1SubBullet1": "Art-Net does not replace DMX: it transports it over IP.",
  "aboutArtnetSection1SubBullet2": "Stability often depends more on the network (switch, Wi-Fi, broadcast) than on the protocol itself.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2PillUniverse": "Universe",
  "aboutArtnetSection2PillNodePort": "Node / Port",
  "aboutArtnetSection2PillMapping": "Mapping",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Paragraph": "Your goal: make sure each universe sent by the source reaches the right node (and the right DMX port) without ambiguity. Most Art-Net failures = wrong universe, wrong subnet/net, or broadcast saturation.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2CalloutTitle": "Simple method (avoids 80% of errors)",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Bullet1": "1) Write it down: “Universe 1 → Node A port 1”, “Universe 2 → Node A port 2”, etc.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Bullet2": "2) Use stable IPs (reserved DHCP or static).",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Bullet3": "3) Prefer unicast if you have more than one node or a shared network.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Subtitle1": "Broadcast vs Unicast",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet1": "Broadcast: you send to everyone → easy but can flood a switch.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet2": "Unicast: you send to the node IP → cleaner and more predictable.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet3": "In show conditions: if in doubt → unicast.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2Subtitle2": "Universes: field practice",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet4": "Keep numbering simple (e.g. 1…N).",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet5": "Avoid “one universe everywhere”: document your mappings.",
  "aboutArtnetSection2SubBullet6": "On some setups, “Net/Subnet/Universe” exist: think “mapping”, not “magic”.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3CalloutTitle": "The real limits (in order)",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet1": "Wi-Fi: variable latency + losses → flicker or dropouts.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet2": "Broadcast: overloads a LAN (everyone receives everything).",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet3": "Low-end switch: limited buffers, poor multicast/broadcast handling.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet4": "Nodes: firmware/CPU limits (number of universes, rate, options).",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Bullet5": "PC/source: driver, CPU load, antivirus, network card, etc.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Subtitle1": "Refresh rate",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet1": "Classic DMX is often around ~20–44 fps depending on frame size.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet2": "Over IP, you can send more, but nodes do not always keep up (and it is not always useful).",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet3": "Stability > frequency: stable at 30–40 fps is better than “fast” but unstable.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3Subtitle2": "Simple rules",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet4": "Dedicated network (or VLAN).",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet5": "Unicast if you have multiple nodes.",
  "aboutArtnetSection3SubBullet6": "Wired for critical shows.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Paragraph": "A node = IP → DMX. It can have 1, 2, 4, 8 DMX ports. Stability depends on the node AND the DMX cabling on the output side (termination, topology, splitters).",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Subtitle1": "Nodes vs Splitters",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet1": "Node: converts IP→DMX (and sometimes DMX→IP).",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet2": "DMX splitter: distributes one DMX line into branches (opto recommended).",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet3": "Good design: IP as close as possible, then short and clean DMX.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4CalloutTitle": "RDM over Art-Net (variable support)",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Bullet1": "Some nodes support an “RDM proxy”: the console sees/configures RDM devices over IP.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Bullet2": "Others do not support RDM at all, or only partially.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Bullet3": "A classic opto splitter can block RDM return (depending on model).",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Bullet4": "Tip: if you want RDM, explicitly choose hardware marked as RDM-compatible.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4Subtitle2": "Good field reflex",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet4": "If DMX is unstable: solve DMX first (termination, cable, passive Y) then IP.",
  "aboutArtnetSection4SubBullet5": "If RDM is unstable: disable RDM to verify pure DMX is stable.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5CalloutTitle": "Symptoms → likely causes",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet1": "Flicker across the whole stage → Wi-Fi, broadcast flood, saturated switch.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet2": "One node works, the other does not → universe/mapping, IP, badly configured unicast.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet3": "Random dropouts → faulty RJ45 cable, unstable switch, PC dropping packets.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet4": "Only one DMX port on a node fails → DMX cable, termination, device breaking the line.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Bullet5": "Impossible RDM discovery → node/splitter not RDM compatible, borderline DMX cabling.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5Subtitle": "Quick method",
  "aboutArtnetSection5SubBullet1": "1) Switch to unicast to a single node (simple test).",
  "aboutArtnetSection5SubBullet2": "2) Check universes/mapping (paper + node config).",
  "aboutArtnetSection5SubBullet3": "3) Test direct wired path (PC → switch → node) with a known-good cable.",
  "aboutArtnetSection5SubBullet4": "4) Isolate DMX: node → 1 fixture + termination.",
